WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:03.502
-We're here with Mason Hereford.
Thanks for being with us, Mason.

00:00:03.502 --> 00:00:04.571
-Thank you, thank you.

00:00:04.571 --> 00:00:06.539
-His cookbook
"Turkey and the Wolf:

00:00:06.539 --> 00:00:09.308
Flavor Trippin' in New Orleans"
is on sale now.

00:00:09.308 --> 00:00:10.510
We're so very happy
to have you here.

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:11.678
We're gonna start with a drink,
right?

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:13.312
-We're gonna start with a drink.

00:00:13.312 --> 00:00:15.482
I don't know how to make those,
so we,

00:00:15.482 --> 00:00:18.351
in true New Orleans fashion, are
gonna have a beer and a shot.

00:00:18.351 --> 00:00:20.186
-Alright, start with the shot,
I'm assuming.

00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:22.121
-Got to knock it on the table.
-Okay, great.

00:00:22.121 --> 00:00:24.190
-Take it down.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:25.258
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:25.258 --> 00:00:26.993
Nicely done.
-Beautiful way to start.

00:00:28.861 --> 00:00:30.697
Alright, so, a lot of your --

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:33.032
[ Laughter ]

00:00:33.032 --> 00:00:35.001
-Alright.

00:00:35.001 --> 00:00:37.370
[ Laughter ]

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:44.810
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:46.479
-Ah.

00:00:46.479 --> 00:00:48.180
The restaurant business.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.283
Oh, son of a -- Okay, great.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:50.283 --> 00:00:54.154
Alright, so a lot of these are
spins on Southern classics.

00:00:54.154 --> 00:00:57.223
-Yeah, exactly, but we like --
This is an example of a dish

00:00:57.223 --> 00:00:59.492
that I just could not stomach
as a kid.

00:00:59.492 --> 00:01:00.727
-This is the bologna sandwich.

00:01:00.727 --> 00:01:02.561
-Just a flabby bologna sandwich.

00:01:02.561 --> 00:01:05.765
-So, how did your family make it
for you when you were a kid?

00:01:05.765 --> 00:01:08.568
-Yeah, so, my mom would give us
two slices of white bread.

00:01:08.568 --> 00:01:11.204
I love my mom.
Happy birthday yesterday.

00:01:11.204 --> 00:01:12.872
[ Laughter ]

00:01:12.872 --> 00:01:14.841
And it would just be
yellow mustard,

00:01:14.841 --> 00:01:16.843
American cheese, and bologna.
-Yeah.

00:01:16.843 --> 00:01:18.478
-And it was really sad for all
of us.

00:01:18.478 --> 00:01:20.479
-It's a sad sandwich.
-And in order to choke it down,

00:01:20.479 --> 00:01:22.882
we put potato chips
right in the center.

00:01:22.882 --> 00:01:24.383
-Okay.
-That would give it some life.

00:01:24.383 --> 00:01:25.985
-Sure.
-It was either that or no lunch,

00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:28.254
and, you know,
you got to have lunch.

00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:32.391
[ Laughter ]
So, what we've done is

00:01:32.391 --> 00:01:34.526
we've elevated that thing to
something we really like,

00:01:34.526 --> 00:01:36.061
and we've involved
a lot of people.

00:01:36.061 --> 00:01:39.131
So I brought you some white
bread from New Orleans,

00:01:39.131 --> 00:01:41.000
baked by my friend David Weiss.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.336
You're gonna cut it and butter
it, if you do not mind.

00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:44.970
-Alright, let's do it.
-You'll notice, extra thick.

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:46.839
So, yeah, cut that off
and then go,

00:01:46.839 --> 00:01:48.374
like, you know, thicker than
your thumb.

00:01:48.374 --> 00:01:49.909
-Alright, great.
-So, if you have a thick slice

00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:53.579
of white bread and you grill it
off, it's gonna be crispy

00:01:53.579 --> 00:01:56.082
on the outside and still kind of
chewy and steamed on the center.

00:01:56.082 --> 00:01:58.017
You've done three. I only needed
two, so that's great.

00:01:58.017 --> 00:02:01.020
-Good. I thought we might both
have a sandwich.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:03.022
[ Laughter ]

00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:05.091
-So next you're gonna apply
butter.

00:02:05.091 --> 00:02:07.226
-Okay.
-Apply butter, edge to edge.

00:02:07.226 --> 00:02:08.361
-Edge to edge.
-All over.

00:02:08.361 --> 00:02:10.029
-And I'm assuming
I can be pretty --

00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:11.197
-Well, the butter is
a little cold,

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:13.332
so this is actually
an inconquerable task.

00:02:13.332 --> 00:02:15.635
-Okay. It's actually not bad.
I think it's spreads better --

00:02:15.635 --> 00:02:18.337
-And you did perfect. Here it
is, in the pan, ready to go.

00:02:18.337 --> 00:02:20.239
-Look, we're gonna pretend like
I just did that. Alright, great.

00:02:20.239 --> 00:02:21.474
-We're gonna move on.

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:23.309
Next, we're gonna have you cut
the bologna,

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:24.510
if you do not mind.
-Alright, great.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.046
-This is actually perfect butter
spreading.

00:02:27.046 --> 00:02:28.448
-Do you want to tell me
in advance

00:02:28.448 --> 00:02:30.216
how many slices of bologna
you want?

00:02:30.216 --> 00:02:31.584
[ Laughter ]

00:02:31.584 --> 00:02:34.486
-Let's go with four.
-Okay, great. That, I can do.

00:02:34.486 --> 00:02:36.990
-So, having tried this earlier,

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:38.958
I did find the serrated knife
was easier.

00:02:38.958 --> 00:02:41.227
-Mm.
-So I recommend that.

00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:43.762
-Okay. How thick are these?
-Not that thick.

00:02:43.762 --> 00:02:45.865
Let's go more towards
this little sample one.

00:02:45.865 --> 00:02:47.666
-Alright, great.
-Oh, man, you're good at that
too.

00:02:47.666 --> 00:02:49.001
-Thank you.
-Better than me.

00:02:49.001 --> 00:02:51.303
-I would say, it's like
an eighth of a thumb.

00:02:51.303 --> 00:02:54.507
If we're gonna do it
all in thumbs.

00:02:54.507 --> 00:02:56.843
-Eighth of a thumb?
Half of a thumb.

00:02:56.843 --> 00:02:58.077
[ Laughter ]

00:02:58.077 --> 00:02:59.845
Alright.
-So we fry that?

00:02:59.845 --> 00:03:01.680
-Into the pan we go, yeah.
So, we've got this thing high.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:02.815
It's got enough fat in it,
we don't need to put

00:03:02.815 --> 00:03:04.583
anything in the pan.
-Alright, great.

00:03:04.583 --> 00:03:05.985
Alright, and so we
just did butter

00:03:05.985 --> 00:03:07.286
in the pan for these, right?

00:03:07.286 --> 00:03:09.822
-Yeah, so, what we did is we put
the butter on the outside

00:03:09.822 --> 00:03:12.458
of this so we could have
the exact amount we wanted

00:03:12.458 --> 00:03:13.926
and it would be
all over the bread.

00:03:13.926 --> 00:03:16.028
And as we take it out of the
pan, what we're gonna do is

00:03:16.028 --> 00:03:17.963
we're gonna lean the bread
against each other

00:03:17.963 --> 00:03:19.866
after we toast it.
-Okay.

00:03:19.866 --> 00:03:20.966
-And what we're doing is
we're gonna let

00:03:20.966 --> 00:03:22.902
some airflow go around that.
-Okay.

00:03:22.902 --> 00:03:24.437
-It's science.
-Yeah.

00:03:24.437 --> 00:03:25.705
-It's resting just like a steak,

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:27.239
but perhaps for
a different reason.

00:03:27.239 --> 00:03:29.842
-Most of the scientists I know
dress and look like you.

00:03:29.842 --> 00:03:32.111
[ Laughter ]

00:03:32.111 --> 00:03:33.512
-Stop.

00:03:33.512 --> 00:03:36.148
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:36.148 --> 00:03:37.750
Nailed it. They love that.

00:03:39.318 --> 00:03:41.754
So, what's gonna happen here is,
the outside kind of gets

00:03:41.754 --> 00:03:44.157
the crisp up, and the center is
not so soggy.

00:03:44.157 --> 00:03:45.558
If you just put it down,
it wouldn't have

00:03:45.558 --> 00:03:47.393
the structural integrity it
needs for all

00:03:47.393 --> 00:03:49.928
this girthy bologna that we got.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:49.928 --> 00:03:52.999
-Ooh, that bologna
is looking good.

00:03:52.999 --> 00:03:55.668
Hey, so I want to ask, if you
can, while you're doing this,

00:03:55.668 --> 00:03:59.271
your restaurant, there's two
categories on the menu --

00:03:59.271 --> 00:04:01.073
sandwiches and not sandwiches.

00:04:01.073 --> 00:04:05.578
As a lover of sandwiches, I find
that incredibly alluring,

00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:08.114
how much you respect
and honor the sandwich.

00:04:08.114 --> 00:04:10.216
-Yeah, if you think about it,

00:04:10.216 --> 00:04:12.251
the sandwich is
the perfect vehicle.

00:04:12.251 --> 00:04:14.520
I mean, I have one
that's already ready.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.489
So the sandwich
is the perfect vehicle.

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:17.990
[ Imitates sizzling ]
-Okay, great.

00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:20.125
[ Laughter ]

00:04:20.125 --> 00:04:22.895
-And so you have
a plate of food.

00:04:22.895 --> 00:04:25.130
And the person, the diner,

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:26.466
can do anything they want with
that plate of food.

00:04:26.466 --> 00:04:28.400
You have a sandwich,
you've got every bite

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:31.504
that you've given to the guests,
the diner, preordained.

00:04:31.504 --> 00:04:33.405
You've decided exactly
how they're gonna eat it.

00:04:33.405 --> 00:04:35.308
And every bite has to be
absolutely perfect.

00:04:35.308 --> 00:04:36.676
So it's actually
a lot more pressure

00:04:36.676 --> 00:04:39.545
than being like an actual chef.
-I agree, right, yeah.

00:04:39.545 --> 00:04:41.146
-Alright, so, let's make
the sandwich.

00:04:41.146 --> 00:04:42.215
We're gonna start with
mayonnaise.

00:04:42.215 --> 00:04:44.516
-Okay.
-I'm a Duke's mayonnaise guy.

00:04:44.516 --> 00:04:45.751
I got a Duke's tattoo
right here,

00:04:45.751 --> 00:04:47.019
right next to
a tattoo of my wife.

00:04:47.019 --> 00:04:48.487
-That's great.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:48.487 --> 00:04:50.589
-So, yeah, corner to corner.
You got to get the whole thing.

00:04:50.589 --> 00:04:52.524
-Okay, yeah, I gotcha.
-Yeah, 'cause some people

00:04:52.524 --> 00:04:54.927
just do the center, and you
obviously fall in that category,

00:04:54.927 --> 00:04:56.229
which is fine.
-Okay.

00:04:56.229 --> 00:04:57.597
-No, no, no.

00:04:57.597 --> 00:04:59.331
-Well, buddy.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:59.331 --> 00:05:01.200
Alright, what's this?
-That's mustard.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.469
-Okay.
Ooh, what kind of mustard?

00:05:03.469 --> 00:05:05.037
Or should you just show me
the tattoo?

00:05:05.037 --> 00:05:07.239
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:07.239 --> 00:05:11.310
So, this is my friend Villa's
mother's mustard recipe

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:12.744
that changed my life.
-Great, alright.

00:05:12.744 --> 00:05:14.480
So, we got a mustard.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.882
-Next is a big pile of
"shrettuce."

00:05:16.882 --> 00:05:19.318
Other side. Come on.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:19.318 --> 00:05:20.753
It's fine, it's fine.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:20.753 --> 00:05:22.354
-Yeah, of course it's fine.

00:05:22.354 --> 00:05:23.856
-The lettuce has to be next to
the mayonnaise.

00:05:23.856 --> 00:05:27.192
[ Laughter ]
So, yeah, shrettuce. Get it?

00:05:27.192 --> 00:05:28.961
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
They all got it.

00:05:28.961 --> 00:05:30.029
[ Laughter ]
-Next up --

00:05:30.029 --> 00:05:32.465
-Chips?
-No, no, not yet, not yet.

00:05:32.465 --> 00:05:34.167
Next up, we've got
the cheesy bologna.

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:36.502
-Okay, right.
Bologna goes on the lettuce.

00:05:36.502 --> 00:05:39.272
-Yeah, and then the chips, and
you want a big pile of chips.

00:05:39.272 --> 00:05:41.307
Yeah, there you go. Big.
Maybe even two-handed.

00:05:41.307 --> 00:05:43.910
-Okay. And then put this on
and just smash?

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:46.812
-Yeah, put it on, take a look at
it for a second, admire,

00:05:46.812 --> 00:05:47.813
and then, with the hand --

00:05:47.813 --> 00:05:50.650
Yeah, there you go.

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:52.351
[ Laughter ]

00:05:52.351 --> 00:05:58.724
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:58.724 --> 00:06:01.460
Let's take a bow.

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:02.595
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:02.595 --> 00:06:07.867 line:0%
-[ Mumbling ]

